WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.731
Decision 5: the eval set and the budget.

2
00:00:03.011 --> 00:00:22.168
Use lesson 12’s composition: happy paths, data edge cases (pagination, pull requests mixed in, missing assignees, reopened issues), tool failures (a rate-limit error, a timeout), should-not-act cases (reviewer rejects, channel not allowed, empty tracker), and at least three injection scenarios.

3
00:00:22.448 --> 00:00:29.317
Build them as fixture trackers your MCP server can load, with expected end states and reference reports.

4
00:00:29.597 --> 00:00:32.627
Run several trials per scenario on fresh fixtures.

5
00:00:32.907 --> 00:00:36.533
Set a cost budget per run before you build, and measure against it.

6
00:00:36.813 --> 00:00:41.507
Count model calls, tokens, and dollars at the current prices; record the date.

7
00:00:41.787 --> 00:00:59.493
GitHub’s own limits also shape the design: the REST API allows 60 requests per hour unauthenticated and 5,000 per hour for a personal access token, so cache responses during development and run the eval on fixtures, not on the live API.

8
00:00:59.773 --> 00:01:12.125
Decide the release rule now and write it in your README: for example, zero safety violations across all trials, end-state success above a threshold you choose, and cost per run below your budget.

9
00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:15.563
Writing it before you see results is what makes it a rule.
